      "page_idx": 6,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.553,
            0.048,
            0.862,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "The Baudhāyana-Pythagoras Theorem"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.089,
            0.767,
            0.109
          ],
          "content": "So a terminating decimal cannot have 2 or 2.000... as its square."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.108,
            0.847,
            0.148
          ],
          "content": "Thus, the decimal expansion of $\\sqrt { 2 }$ must go on forever, i.e., it has a non-terminating decimal representation."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.161,
            0.811,
            0.202
          ],
          "content": "？ Can $\\sqrt { 2 }$ be expressed as a fraction $\\frac { m } { n }$ , where m and n are counting numbers?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.824,
            0.145,
            0.926,
            0.208
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.211,
            0.657,
            0.237
          ],
          "content": "If $\\sqrt { 2 }$ could be expressed as $\\frac { m } { n }$ , then we would have"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.447,
            0.241,
            0.522,
            0.267
          ],
          "content": "{ \\sqrt { 2 } } = { \\frac { m } { n } }",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.459,
            0.269,
            0.536,
            0.306
          ],
          "content": "2 = { \\frac { \\; m ^ { 2 } \\; } { n ^ { 2 } } }",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.442,
            0.308,
            0.533,
            0.329
          ],
          "content": "2 n ^ { 2 } = m ^ { 2 } .",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.334,
            0.847,
            0.435
          ],
          "content": "Recall that in the prime factorization of a square number, each prime occurs an even number of times. So in the equation $2 n ^ { 2 } = m ^ { 2 }$ , the prime 2 would occur an odd number of times on the left side and an even number of times on the right side. This is impossible. Thus $\\sqrt { 2 }$ cannot be expressed as a fraction $\\frac { m } { n }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.436,
            0.845,
            0.493
          ],
          "content": "This beautiful proof that $\\sqrt { 2 }$ cannot be expressed as $\\frac { m } { n }$ where m, n are counting numbers was given by Euclid in his book Elements (c. 300 BCE). We will discuss it in more detail in a later class."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.493,
            0.847,
            0.551
          ],
          "content": "Thus the number $\\sqrt { 2 }$ cannot be expressed as a terminating decimal or a fraction. But we can think of it as a certain non-terminating decimal: $\\sqrt{2}=1.41421356.$ …"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.56,
            0.295,
            0.583
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.586,
            0.802,
            0.661
          ],
          "content": "1. Earlier, we saw a method to create a square with double the area of a given square paper. There is another method to do this in which two identical square papers are cut in the following way."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.816,
            0.587,
            0.904,
            0.644
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.317,
            0.656,
            0.47,
            0.76
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
